/**
 * 情境题英文覆盖层：中文是基底，这里按卡片 id 提供英文 front/back。
 * 与 flashcard-i18n.ts（AMC10 概念卡）分开，避免两个卡族的翻译互相牵动；
 * 缺失的条目自动回退中文（localizeFlashcard 会把两层合并）。
 * 生成方式：见 .audit 里记录的翻译批次；结构由 npm run check:flashcards 校验。
 */
export const SCENARIO_EN: Record<string, { front: string; back: string }> = {
  "csa-scenario-001": { front: "ArrayList<Integer> a = new ArrayList<Integer>();\nint[] d = {2, 4, 5, 6, 8};\nfor (int v : d) { a.add(v); }\nfor (int i = 0; i < a.size(); i++) { if (a.get(i) % 2 == 0) { a.remove(i); } }\nWhat is the output of System.out.println(a);, and which loop form makes \"remove all even numbers\" hold?\nA. Output [4, 5, 8]; switching to the remove overload that deletes by element is enough to remove them all\nB. Output [5]; thinks that switching to an enhanced for loop and deleting removes all even numbers\nC. Output [4, 5, 8]; iterating backward from the end while deleting is what removes all even numbers\nD. Output [5]; thinks the original loop already removes all even numbers correctly and needs no change", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A after switching to the by-element remove overload the index still advances, so the 4 that shifts left is skipped; B removing inside an enhanced for throws ConcurrentModificationException; D the actual output still contains the even number 8, showing the even numbers are not all removed.\n\nCorrect option: Output [4, 5, 8]; iterating backward from the end while deleting is what removes all even numbers" },
  "csa-scenario-002": { front: "static void modify(ArrayList<Integer> p) {\np.add(0, 99);\np = new ArrayList<Integer>();\np.add(7);\n}\nArrayList<Integer> a = new ArrayList<Integer>();\na.add(1);\na.add(2);\nmodify(a);\nSystem.out.println(a);\nA. Output [99, 1, 2]; p and a refer to the same list, so reassigning only changes the local reference\nB. Output [99, 1, 2, 7]; p is always an alias of a, so both add calls write into the same list\nC. Output [1, 2]; thinks that modifying the parameter reference inside the method does not carry back to the caller's list\nD. Output [7, 99, 1, 2]; p = new is the same as clearing a and then inserting 7", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B wrongly assumes that p still refers to a after being reassigned; C ignores that p.add(0, 99) has already modified a; D misreads p = new as clearing the original list and then inserting.\n\nCorrect option: Output [99, 1, 2]; p and a refer to the same list, so reassigning only changes the local reference" },
  "csa-scenario-003": { front: "int[][] t = {{5, 1}, {2, 7, 3}};\nint s = 0;\nfor (int r = 0; r < t.length; r++) {\nfor (int c = 0; c <= t[r].length; c++) {\ns += t[r][c];\n}\n}\nWhat is the result of running System.out.println(s);?\nA. Outputs 18; the default value 0 read at the out-of-bounds position does not affect the total\nB. Outputs 15; the inner condition is equivalent to iterating both rows using the length of the first row\nC. Compile error; a ragged two-dimensional array initialization is not legal in Java\nD. Throws an out-of-bounds exception at run time; when r=0, c reaches the illegal index 2", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A an out-of-bounds access throws an exception directly, so the default value 0 is not read; B the inner condition is c <= t[r].length and does not truncate to the length of the first row; C this initialization of a jagged array is completely legal in Java.\n\nCorrect option: Throws an out-of-bounds exception at run time; when r=0, c reaches the illegal index 2" },
  "csa-scenario-004": { front: "int[][] g = new int[2][2];\nint[] row = g[0];\nrow[0] = 8;\ng[1] = row;\nrow[1] = 9;\nSystem.out.println(g[0][0] + \" \" + g[1][1]);\nA. Output 0 0; assignment between arrays always copies out a new array\nB. Output 8 9; the two rows are the same array, so a change through row is visible in both places\nC. Output 0 9; row is a copied row and is no longer linked to the row g[0]\nD. Output 8 0; g[1] = row copied the contents at that time, and afterwards they do not affect each other", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A array assignment does not copy elements, so the two variables refer to the same array; C ignores that int[] row = g[0] assigns a reference; D ignores that after g[1] = row the two rows share the same array.\n\nCorrect option: Output 8 9; the two rows are the same array, so a change through row is visible in both places" },
  "csa-scenario-005": { front: "static void fill(int[] arr) {\nfor (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) { arr[i] = arr[i] + 1; }\n}\nstatic void replace(int[] arr) { arr = new int[]{0, 0, 0}; }\nint[] a = {1, 2, 3};\nfill(a);\nreplace(a);\nSystem.out.println(a[0] + \",\" + a[2]);\nA. Output 2,4; fill changed the array, replace only changed the local reference\nB. Output 0,0; thinks the array newly created inside replace was also assigned to the caller's a\nC. Output 1,3; modifications to array elements inside a method do not affect the caller's array\nD. Compile error; method parameters are implicitly declared final, so reassignment is not allowed", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B replace only changed the parameter reference and did not write back to a; C fill modifies elements through the reference, so it does affect the caller's array; D Java method parameters are not final, so reassignment is completely legal.\n\nCorrect option: Output 2,4; fill changed the array, replace only changed the local reference" },
  "csa-scenario-006": { front: "int[] a = {3, 1, 4};\nfor (int x : a) { x = x * 2; }\nThe student wanted to double every element. Which choice is correct about the array's final contents and the right fix?\nA. {6, 2, 8}; thinks x is an alias of the array element, so everything is doubled when the loop ends\nB. {3, 1, 4}; should be changed to a[x] = x * 2, because x was treated as the index of the element\nC. Compile error; thinks the loop variable of an enhanced for cannot be reassigned inside the loop body\nD. {3, 1, 4}; x is only a copy, so a[i] = a[i] * 2 must be used instead", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A the x of an enhanced for has no referential relationship with the array element; B x is the element value, not the index, so a[x] goes out of bounds; C this code compiles normally, it simply does not change the array.\n\nCorrect option: {3, 1, 4}; x is only a copy, so a[i] = a[i] * 2 must be used instead" },
  "csa-scenario-007": { front: "ArrayList<String> w = new ArrayList<String>();\nw.add(\"a\");\nw.add(\"bb\");\nw.add(\"ccc\");\nArrayList<String> r = new ArrayList<String>();\nfor (String s : w) { if (s.length() > 1) { r.add(0, s); } }\nSystem.out.println(r);\nA. [bb, ccc]; treats add(0, s) as appending the element at the end of the list\nB. [ccc, bb]; keeps only the strings whose length is greater than 1, and inserts each one at index 0\nC. [ccc]; treats add(0, s) as replacing the element at index 0\nD. Throws an out-of-bounds exception; thinks inserting at index 0 in an empty list is an illegal operation", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A add(0, s) inserts rather than appends at the end; C add is not set and does not replace an existing element; D inserting at index 0 in an empty list is legal and does not throw an exception.\n\nCorrect option: [ccc, bb]; keeps only the strings whose length is greater than 1, and inserts each one at index 0" },
  "csa-scenario-008": { front: "class Animal { public String speak() { return \"?\"; } }\nclass Dog extends Animal { public String speak() { return \"woof\"; } public String fetch() { return \"ball\"; } }\nAnimal a = new Dog();\nDog d = (Dog) a;\nSystem.out.println(a.speak() + d.fetch());\nA. ?ball; thinks the declared type of variable a determines which version of speak is finally called\nB. Compile error; thinks a superclass reference must be downcast before the speak method can be called\nC. woofball; speak is bound according to the run-time object, and fetch can be called after the cast\nD. Throws a type-cast exception at run time; thinks casting a superclass reference to a subclass must fail", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A an overridden method selects its version by run-time type, independent of the declared type; B a superclass reference can call speak directly, without casting first; D the actual object is a Dog, so the downcast does not fail.\n\nCorrect option: woofball; speak is bound according to the run-time object, and fetch can be called after the cast" },
  "csa-scenario-009": { front: "class Shape { public double area() { return 0; } public String name() { return \"shape\"; } }\nclass Square extends Shape { public double area() { return 4; } }\nclass Circle extends Shape { public double area() { return 3; } public String name() { return \"circle\"; } }\nShape[] s = { new Square(), new Circle(), new Shape() };\ndouble t = 0;\nfor (Shape p : s) { t += p.area(); }\nSystem.out.println(t + \" \" + s[1].name());\nA. 7.0 circle; area is called according to the run-time object, and name is overridden too\nB. 0.0 circle; a Shape variable only calls the area implementation defined in the superclass\nC. 7.0 shape; name was not overridden, so the string defined in the superclass is printed\nD. Throws a type-cast exception at run time; because iterating a heterogeneous array always requires downcasting", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B dynamic binding calls the area overridden in the subclass; C name has already been overridden in Circle; D calling an overridden method through a superclass reference does not require downcasting.\n\nCorrect option: 7.0 circle; area is called according to the run-time object, and name is overridden too" },
  "csa-scenario-010": { front: "public class Ticket {\nprivate static int sold = 0;\nprivate int id;\npublic Ticket() { sold++; id = sold; }\npublic int getId() { return id; }\npublic static int getSold() { return sold; }\n}\nTicket a = new Ticket();\nTicket b = new Ticket();\nSystem.out.println(a.getId() + \" \" + b.getId() + \" \" + Ticket.getSold());\nA. 0 1 2; id = sold executes first and then sold++, so the numbering starts at 0\nB. 2 2 2; thinks id is also declared static, so the two objects share the same number\nC. Compile error; thinks the class's static field sold cannot be accessed inside a constructor body\nD. 1 2 2; sold is shared by all objects, and each construction increments it before assigning it to id", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A the code increments first and then assigns, so the numbering starts at 1; B id is an instance field, and each object has its own copy; C accessing a static field inside a constructor is completely legal.\n\nCorrect option: 1 2 2; sold is shared by all objects, and each construction increments it before assigning it to id" },
  "csa-scenario-011": { front: "class P {\npublic P() { System.out.print(\"P\"); }\npublic P(int x) { System.out.print(\"P\" + x); }\n}\nclass C extends P {\npublic C() { super(2); System.out.print(\"C\"); }\n}\nnew C();\nA. PC; because super(2) finds no match and falls back to calling the superclass no-argument constructor\nB. P2C; the superclass constructor with an argument runs first and prints P2, then the subclass body prints C\nC. CP2; thinks the subclass constructor body finishes first, and then the superclass constructor runs\nD. Compile error; thinks a subclass constructor can only call the no-argument constructor through super()", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A an explicit super(2) can match the superclass constructor with a parameter and does not fall back to the no-argument version; C the superclass constructor always executes before the subclass constructor body; D a subclass constructor may call the superclass constructor that takes an argument.\n\nCorrect option: P2C; the superclass constructor with an argument runs first and prints P2, then the subclass body prints C" },
  "csa-scenario-012": { front: "abstract class Food {\npublic abstract double cost();\n}\nclass Pizza extends Food {\nprivate int slices;\npublic Pizza(int n) { slices = n; }\npublic double cost(int n) { return n * 3.0; }\n}\nFood f = new Pizza(4);\nSystem.out.println(f.cost());\nA. Throws AbstractMethodError at run time; f.cost() finds no implementation\nB. Compile error; thinks the error is at the call site, and f must first be cast to Pizza\nC. Compile error; cost(int) is an overload, not an override, and should be changed to a no-argument cost()\nD. No error; thinks the same method name counts as implementing the abstract method, and 12.0 is printed", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A an unimplemented abstract method is a compile-time error and is not deferred to run time; B the error is in the class declaration of Pizza, and a cast cannot solve it; D the same method name is not an override, and a different parameter list is only an overload.\n\nCorrect option: Compile error; cost(int) is an overload, not an override, and should be changed to a no-argument cost()" },
  "csa-scenario-013": { front: "public static int f(int n) {\nif (n <= 1) { return n; }\nreturn f(n - 1) + f(n - 2);\n}\nAfter calling f(4), what are the return value and the total number of times f is called (including the initial call)?\nA. Return value 3, f is called 9 times in total, including the initial f(4) call\nB. Return value 5, f is called 9 times in total, because n <= 1 is treated as always returning 1\nC. Return value 3, f is called 8 times in total, because the initial call is not counted in the total\nD. Return value 3, f is called 7 times in total, because two f(0) calls were missed", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B the return values of the base case are not all 1, since f(0) returns 0; C the initial f(4) call must also be counted in the total; D f(0) really is called twice and must not be missed.\n\nCorrect option: Return value 3, f is called 9 times in total, including the initial f(4) call" },
  "csa-scenario-014": { front: "public static String g(String s) {\nif (s.length() <= 1) { return s; }\nreturn g(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0);\n}\nWhat is the return value of g(\"abc\")?\nA. bca; only the outermost concatenation is done, without continuing to recurse on the substring\nB. abc; the concatenation order is reversed, written as charAt(0) + g(s)\nC. cba; each level first reverses the remaining substring and then appends the first character to the end of the result\nD. Throws an out-of-bounds exception; thinks calling charAt(0) on the empty string also fails out of bounds", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A the code keeps recursing on the substring and does not do only the outermost step; B the concatenation order puts the recursive result first, not charAt(0); D the base case catches the empty string, so charAt(0) is never called.\n\nCorrect option: cba; each level first reverses the remaining substring and then appends the first character to the end of the result" },
  "csa-scenario-015": { front: "public static int h(int n) {\nif (n == 0) { return 0; }\nreturn n + h(n - 2);\n}\nWhat is the result of h(5)?\nA. Returns 9; thinks the termination condition is automatically satisfied when n decreases to 1\nB. Throws a stack overflow error; an odd n skips 0, so the recursion never terminates\nC. Returns 15; ignores the step of 2 and treats h as computing the sum of consecutive integers from 1 to 5\nD. Returns 8; thinks the last remaining h(1) is not included in the result and only 5 and 3 are added", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A n == 0 is the only termination condition, and an odd sequence never reaches 0; C the step is 2, so it does not add 1 through 5 one by one; D the recursion never ends at all, so there is no remaining h(1) result.\n\nCorrect option: Throws a stack overflow error; an odd n skips 0, so the recursion never terminates" },
  "csa-scenario-016": { front: "public static int m(int[] a, int i) {\nif (i == a.length) { return 0; }\nint rest = m(a, i + 1);\nreturn a[i] > rest ? a[i] : rest;\n}\nint[] d = {3, 9, 4};\nint r = m(d, 0); // what is the value of r?\nA. 0; thinks the ternary expression finally returns the value of the base case\nB. 3; thinks there is no comparison and the value of the first element a[0] is returned directly\nC. 4; thinks the recursion always returns the value of the array's last element\nD. 9; each level compares the current element with the maximum of the right-hand subproblem and returns it", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A the base case only serves as the initial comparison value, and the maximum is what is finally returned; B every level performs a comparison and does not return a[0] directly; C the return value is determined by the comparison result and is unrelated to the last element.\n\nCorrect option: 9; each level compares the current element with the maximum of the right-hand subproblem and returns it" },
  "csa-scenario-017": { front: "public static void p(int n) {\nif (n == 0) { return; }\np(n - 1);\nSystem.out.print(n);\np(n - 1);\n}\nWhat is the output of p(2);?\nA. 211; places the print statement before the two recursive calls, printing first and then recursing\nB. 121; first goes through the left recursion down to the base case, then prints n, then goes through the right recursion\nC. 12; omits the last line's p(n - 1) call and goes through only the left half of the recursion\nD. 112; places the print statement after the two recursive calls, printing only after both recursions finish", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A writing the print before the recursion would output 2 first; C omitting the last p(n - 1) would output one fewer 1; D writing the print after the two recursions would give 112.\n\nCorrect option: 121; first goes through the left recursion down to the base case, then prints n, then goes through the right recursion" },
  "csa-scenario-018": { front: "int[] a = {5, 3, 8, 1, 9};\nfor (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\nint min = i;\nfor (int j = i + 1; j < a.length; j++) { if (a[j] < a[min]) { min = j; } }\nint t = a[i]; a[i] = a[min]; a[min] = t;\n}\nWhat are the contents of the array after the loop ends, and how many more swaps are needed to make it fully ascending?\nA. {1, 3, 8, 5, 9}, 1 more swap needed; when i=2, 5 is swapped into index 2\nB. {1, 3, 8, 5, 9}, 2 more swaps needed; thinks both remaining passes necessarily require a swap\nC. {1, 3, 5, 8, 9}, 0 more swaps needed; thinks two passes have already put the first three positions in order\nD. {1, 1, 8, 1, 9}, 1 more swap needed; thinks assigning alone moves the minimum to the front", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B among the two remaining passes only i=2 requires a swap; C two passes fix only the first two positions and the array is not sorted; D the code uses a three-variable swap and does not overwrite element 5 with an assignment.\n\nCorrect option: {1, 3, 8, 5, 9}, 1 more swap needed; when i=2, 5 is swapped into index 2" },
  "csa-scenario-019": { front: "int[] a = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14};\n// binary search for 12: lo=0, hi=a.length-1, mid=(lo+hi)/2,\n// each time a[mid] is compared with the target, and it stops on a hit\nWhat are the successive mid indices and the total number of comparisons?\nA. 3, 4, 5, 3 comparisons in total; lo is written as mid instead of mid + 1, adding an extra pass\nB. 3, 5, 3 comparisons in total; after hitting 12, one more comparison with an adjacent element is made for confirmation\nC. 4, 6, 2 comparisons in total; indices are numbered starting from 1, and (1 + 7) / 2 gives 4\nD. 3, 5, 2 comparisons in total; first a[3]=8 is compared and then a[5]=12, hitting on the second comparison", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A writing lo as mid adds an extra comparison pass and produces an extra mid of 4; B the search stops immediately on a hit, so no adjacent element needs to be compared; C array indices start at 0, so mid cannot be 4 or 6.\n\nCorrect option: 3, 5, 2 comparisons in total; first a[3]=8 is compared and then a[5]=12, hitting on the second comparison" },
  "csa-scenario-020": { front: "int[] a = {4, 2, 7, 1};\n// insertion sort: the outer i goes from 1 to a.length-1, inserting a[i] into the sorted prefix,\n// and each element shifted one position to the right counts as 1 right shift\nWhat are the contents of the array after the i = 2 pass finishes, and the total number of right shifts for the whole sort?\nA. {1, 2, 4, 7}, 4 right shifts in total; thinks the array is completely sorted after i=2 finishes\nB. {2, 4, 1, 7}, 4 right shifts in total; thinks each pass swaps positions with the adjacent element on the right\nC. {2, 4, 7, 1}, 4 right shifts in total; when i=2, 7 is already in place and only 1 needs to move\nD. {2, 4, 7, 1}, 5 right shifts in total; treats the number of comparisons as the number of right shifts (1+1+3=5), confusing comparisons with moves", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A when i=2 finishes, 1 has not been inserted yet and the array is not sorted; B insertion sort shifts elements right to make room, it does not swap adjacent elements; D the number of right shifts is 1+0+3=4, and 5 is the number of comparisons.\n\nCorrect option: {2, 4, 7, 1}, 4 right shifts in total; when i=2, 7 is already in place and only 1 needs to move" },
  "csa-scenario-021": { front: "public static int count(int[] a, int t) {\nint c = 0;\nfor (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {\nif (a[i] == t) { c++; return c; }\n}\nreturn c;\n}\nint[] d = {3, 5, 3, 5};\nWhat is the return value of count(d, 3), and what is the correct fix?\nA. Returns 1; this does not match the expected 2, so return c should be moved outside the loop\nB. Returns 2, matching the expectation; the loop keeps comparing later elements and accumulating the count\nC. Compile error; thinks there is an execution path with no return, so the method is missing a return value\nD. Returns 1; c++ should be changed to c = c + 1 so that the increment takes effect earlier", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B the return is written inside the loop body, so the method ends on the first hit; C the method already has a return at the end and compiles; D the c++ increment does take effect, and the real problem is the early return.\n\nCorrect option: Returns 1; this does not match the expected 2, so return c should be moved outside the loop" },
  "csa-scenario-022": { front: "int a = 7, b = 2;\ndouble r = a / b;\ndouble s = (double) a / b;\nSystem.out.println(r + \" \" + s);\nA. 3.5 3.5; thinks assigning the result to a double makes the right-hand side evaluate as double first\nB. 3.0 3.5; the first line does integer division first and then widens, and the second line yields a decimal after the cast\nC. 3 3.5; thinks a double variable prints without a decimal point when it stores an integer value\nD. 3.5 3.0; reverses where (double) applies, thinking only the first line is promoted", back: "Answer: B\nOne-line analysis: A the type of the assignment target does not affect the integer division on the right-hand side; C a double variable still prints a decimal point when it holds an integer value; D the promotion happens only on the second line, and the first line is still integer division.\n\nCorrect option: 3.0 3.5; the first line does integer division first and then widens, and the second line yields a decimal after the cast" },
  "csa-scenario-023": { front: "char c = 'A';\nint k = c + 1;\nchar d = (char) k;\nSystem.out.println(k + \" \" + d);\nA. 66 A; thinks the (char) narrowing conversion does not change the character and d is still 'A'\nB. 66 66; thinks a char variable displays the numeric code when printed with println\nC. 66 B; 'A' is encoded as 65, and after adding 1 and converting back to char it is B\nD. Compile error; thinks a char must first be explicitly converted to int before arithmetic with an int", back: "Answer: C\nOne-line analysis: A the (char) cast really does convert 66 to the character B; B printing a char variable with println outputs the character itself; D mixed arithmetic between char and int promotes automatically and needs no explicit conversion.\n\nCorrect option: 66 B; 'A' is encoded as 65, and after adding 1 and converting back to char it is B" },
  "csa-scenario-024": { front: "int i = 0;\nboolean b = (i++ > 0) && (i++ > 1);\nSystem.out.println(i + \" \" + b);\nA. 2 false; thinks both sides of && are evaluated and both increments execute\nB. 2 true; treats the postfix i++ as the prefix ++i, so both conditions hold\nC. 0 false; thinks the increments inside do not take effect when the whole boolean expression fails\nD. 1 false; the left side is false and short-circuits, so the second increment no longer executes", back: "Answer: D\nOne-line analysis: A when the left side of && is false the right side is not evaluated at all; B i++ compares with the old value 0 first, so the left side is false; C the i++ on the left has already executed, and i does not return to 0.\n\nCorrect option: 1 false; the left side is false and short-circuits, so the second increment no longer executes" },
  "csa-scenario-025": { front: "int i = 0, sum = 0;\nwhile (i < 5) {\ni++;\nif (i % 2 == 0) { continue; }\nsum += i;\n}\nSystem.out.println(sum + \" \" + i);\nA. 9 5; only the odd numbers 1, 3, 5 are accumulated, and the loop exits after i reaches 5\nB. 15 5; ignores the if and continue and accumulates all of 1 through 5\nC. 1 2; treats continue as break and exits the loop on an even number\nD. 6 5; reverses the modulus condition and adds only the even numbers 2 and 4", back: "Answer: A\nOne-line analysis: B continue skips the even numbers, it does not add all of 1 through 5; C continue skips only the current iteration and does not end the loop; D the condition skips even numbers, so the odd numbers are accumulated.\n\nCorrect option: 9 5; only the odd numbers 1, 3, 5 are accumulated, and the loop exits after i reaches 5" },
  "csp-scenario-001": { front: "a←[3,7,2,9]\nAPPEND(a,5)\nINSERT(a,2,6)\nREMOVE(a,4)\nList indices start at 1. After execution, what are the contents of a?\nA. The result becomes [3,6,7,2,9,5]\nB. The result becomes [3,6,7,2,5]\nC. The result becomes [3,6,7,9,5]\nD. The result becomes [3,6,7,2,9]", back: "Answer: C\nA mistakenly treats REMOVE as deleting by value (the list contains no 4, so nothing is removed), B treats the value 9 in the list after the insertion as the element to remove (confusing an index with an element value), and D mistakenly believes that REMOVE deletes the last element.\n\nCorrect option: The result becomes [3,6,7,9,5]" },
  "csp-scenario-002": { front: "t←0\nFOR EACH x IN [2,5,3,80]\nIF (x MOD 2) = 0\nt←t+x\nELSE\nt←t-x\nAfter execution, what is the value of t?\nA. t equals 74\nB. t equals -74\nC. t equals 82\nD. t equals 90", back: "Answer: A\nB reverses the condition (even numbers subtracted, odd numbers added) and gets -74, C ignores the ELSE branch and only adds the even numbers, getting 82, and D ignores the condition entirely and adds all 4 elements, getting 90.\n\nCorrect option: t equals 74" },
  "csp-scenario-003": { front: "The daily screen time in hours of the 11 students in a class, sorted, is 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6, 48, where 48 is a data entry error. If 48 is changed to 4, which of the following is correct?\nA. Neither the median nor the mean changes, because the number of data values has not changed\nB. Both the median and the mean decrease, and the median decreases more\nC. The median decreases by about 2 and the mean decreases by about 4 hours\nD. The median stays unchanged and the mean decreases by about 4 hours", back: "Answer: D\nA sees only that the number of data values has not changed and concludes that neither statistic changes, B mistakenly believes that the median is more easily pulled by extreme values than the mean, and C mistakenly believes that the median changes (after 48 is changed to 4, the median is still 3).\n\nCorrect option: The median stays unchanged and the mean decreases by about 4 hours" },
  "csp-scenario-004": { front: "A school wants to estimate the average sleep duration of all 1200 students. On a school day at 7:30 the surveyor asks only the 100 students who arrive at school earliest, and distributes the questionnaire only through the campus app. Which of the following is correct about this sample?\nA. The sample has only 100 people, so expanding it to 300 people would eliminate the bias\nB. The sample is not representative, and students who arrive early and have the app installed may sleep less\nC. This is random sampling among students, so the results can be used to infer a causal relationship\nD. The questionnaire is collected through the campus app and transmitted encrypted, so the data are relatively objective and reliable", back: "Answer: B\nA assumes that sample size can eliminate bias, C mistakes a convenience sample for random sampling and further infers causation, and D mistakes encrypted transmission for data that are objective and reliable.\n\nCorrect option: The sample is not representative, and students who arrive early and have the app installed may sleep less" },
  "csp-scenario-005": { front: "g←[58,72,91,45,88]\nt←0\nc←0\nFOR EACH x IN g\nIF x ≥ 60\nt←t+x\nc←c+1\nDISPLAY(t/c)\nThe program outputs about 83.7. What does this output represent?\nA. The arithmetic mean of the scores of all 5 students\nB. The sum of the scores of the 3 students who scored 60 or above\nC. The average score of the 3 students who scored 60 or above\nD. The proportion of the total score of the students who scored 60 or above to the total score of the whole class", back: "Answer: C\nA ignores the IF condition (the class average is about 70.8), B forgets the final division and looks only at the accumulated sum 251, and D reads the numerator and denominator as the passing total and the class total (a ratio of about 70.9%).\n\nCorrect option: The average score of the 3 students who scored 60 or above" },
  "csp-scenario-006": { front: "An image is 50×100 pixels, and the brightness of each pixel is represented with 4 bits and stored uncompressed. Which of the following is correct about this image?\nA. 2500 bytes are needed, and brightness has 16 possible values\nB. 20000 bytes are needed, and brightness has 16 possible values\nC. 2500 bytes are needed, and brightness has 4 possible values\nD. 5000 bytes are needed, and brightness has 256 possible values", back: "Answer: A\nB treats 4 bits as 4 bytes and computes 20000 bytes, C treats the bit depth directly as the number of values (mistakenly writing 16 values as 4 values), and D ignores the bit depth, computes at 1 byte per pixel, and wrongly uses the 8-bit number of colors.\n\nCorrect option: 2500 bytes are needed, and brightness has 16 possible values" },
  "csp-scenario-007": { front: "A remote surgery system requires the round-trip latency of control commands to be below 50 milliseconds and needs about 20 Mbps of bandwidth to send video back. The carrier raises the bandwidth from 20 Mbps to 100 Mbps, and the round-trip latency between the two locations does not change. Which of the following is correct?\nA. After the bandwidth is raised, the latency decreases accordingly and the controls feel noticeably better\nB. Higher bandwidth means that packet loss no longer occurs, so transmission between the two ends is necessarily reliable\nC. Latency is determined by the distance between the two locations, and no technical means can reduce it\nD. The returned video can be clearer, but the controls will not feel better", back: "Answer: D\nA is the typical misconception that \"large bandwidth means low latency\", B conflates bandwidth with reliability (bandwidth does not eliminate packet loss), and C ignores means such as route optimization and edge nodes that can indeed reduce latency.\n\nCorrect option: The returned video can be clearer, but the controls will not feel better" },
  "csp-scenario-008": { front: "A message is split into 5 packets and sent over a packet-switching network. The 3rd packet is lost on the way, and the receiver receives the other 4 packets. Which of the following is correct?\nA. As long as redundant paths exist in the network, no packet is ever lost, so the receiver does not need to request retransmission\nB. The lost packet can be retransmitted over another path, and the receiver reassembles the original message by sequence number\nC. Packets must arrive along the same path in the order they were sent, otherwise the whole message must be discarded\nD. The sender must resend all 5 packets once in order to restore the whole message", back: "Answer: B\nA mistakes redundant paths for never losing packets, C mistakenly believes that packets must travel the same path and arrive in strict order, and D mistakenly believes that losing one packet requires resending all the data.\n\nCorrect option: The lost packet can be retransmitted over another path, and the receiver reassembles the original message by sequence number" },
  "csp-scenario-009": { front: "A student types school.example.edu into the browser and the page opens successfully. Which of the following is correct about this access?\nA. The browser communicates with the server directly using the domain name, and the IP address is used only when resolution fails\nB. TCP/IP is responsible for rendering the web page into an image, and HTTP is responsible for transmitting the bits of the web page\nC. The domain name is first resolved to an IP address by DNS, and the browser then uses it to request the page content\nD. One domain name can correspond to only one IP address, so every access connects to the same server", back: "Answer: C\nA assumes that the domain name can be used for communication directly, B swaps the responsibilities of TCP/IP and HTTP and also mixes in rendering, and D mistakenly believes that one domain name can correspond to only one IP address (ignoring load balancing and multiple servers).\n\nCorrect option: The domain name is first resolved to an IP address by DNS, and the browser then uses it to request the page content" },
  "csp-scenario-010": { front: "A student wants to upload a 120 MB assignment to a server. The upload bandwidth is 10 Mbps; ignore protocol overhead (1 byte = 8 bits). About how long does the upload take?\nA. About 96 seconds\nB. About 12 seconds\nC. About 960 seconds\nD. About 9600 seconds", back: "Answer: A\nB ignores the byte-to-bit conversion and computes 120÷10 directly, C converts the units but forgets to divide by the bandwidth and is left with only 960, and D writes the division as a multiplication and gets 960×10.\n\nCorrect option: About 96 seconds" },
  "csp-scenario-011": { front: "A hospital stores medical records in 3 data centers at the same time. When 1 of them goes down because of a power outage, the system can still access the data, but the annual maintenance cost rises noticeably. Which of the following is correct?\nA. This reflects encryption, because once the data are stored in separate places others cannot read their contents\nB. Multiple copies share the same data, so the total storage and maintenance cost will not rise\nC. This reflects fault tolerance, and as long as there are multiple copies the data will not be modified incorrectly\nD. This reflects fault tolerance; the data remain accessible when a single data center fails, but the cost rises", back: "Answer: D\nA mistakes redundancy for encryption, B mistakenly believes that copies take no extra storage or cost (copies actually increase the total overhead), and C is wrong in believing that redundancy can guarantee the correctness of the data content.\n\nCorrect option: This reflects fault tolerance; the data remain accessible when a single data center fails, but the cost rises" },
  "csp-scenario-012": { front: "A campus app collects the following data: student ID, date of birth, total steps per day, email address, and the classroom number of each class each day. Which of the following is correct?\nA. Only names count as personally identifiable information; student IDs and email addresses are just ordinary numbers\nB. Student IDs are personally identifiable information, and a combination of classroom numbers may also identify an individual\nC. As long as no names are collected, these data are no longer personal information and can be made public\nD. Step counts are also personally identifiable information, because they are stored in numeric form", back: "Answer: B\nA equates PII with names, C assumes that removing names is the same as de-identification, and D treats the format of the data (numbers) as the basis for judging sensitivity.\n\nCorrect option: Student IDs are personally identifiable information, and a combination of classroom numbers may also identify an individual" },
  "csp-scenario-013": { front: "A website uses HTTPS to transmit login information, and the server stores only the hash of \"password + random salt\" and compares hash values at login. Which of the following is correct?\nA. A hash is a form of reversible encryption, and the server can use a key to restore the hash to the original password\nB. HTTPS can guarantee that attackers cannot read the passwords in the database, so hashing is no longer necessary\nC. The transmission is protected by HTTPS, and even if the database is leaked the hash makes the password hard to recover\nD. The purpose of adding salt is to make the same password produce the same hash value every time, which makes comparison easier", back: "Answer: C\nA treats the irreversible hash as reversible encryption, B extends the scope of protection of transmission encryption to the server database, and D misunderstands salting as keeping the hash fixed (salting is precisely what makes each result different).\n\nCorrect option: The transmission is protected by HTTPS, and even if the database is leaked the hash makes the password hard to recover" },
  "csp-scenario-014": { front: "A student logs in to the campus system with a \"password + phone verification code\", and the system also restricts him by role so that he can view only his own grades. Which of the following is correct?\nA. The password and phone verification code are used for authentication, and restricting the viewing scope is authorization\nB. The verification code is authorization, because it determines which pages you can see\nC. Both are encryption measures, because both protect the confidentiality of the data\nD. Two-factor authentication guarantees that the account cannot be cracked, so a strong password is no longer needed", back: "Answer: A\nB mistakes an authentication method for authorization, C mistakes authentication for encryption (encryption does not determine identity), and D exaggerates the effect of two-factor authentication and on that basis gives up strong passwords.\n\nCorrect option: The password and phone verification code are used for authentication, and restricting the viewing scope is authorization" },
  "csp-scenario-015": { front: "A team uses records from the past 5 years to train a model that predicts whether a student needs tutoring. Because low-income students have received less tutoring in the past, the model's predictions for these students are low. Which of the following is correct?\nA. As long as the household income field is deleted, the output of the model will no longer be biased\nB. The bias shows that the amount of data is insufficient; increasing the records 10-fold will eliminate it\nC. A model is only a mathematical computation, so it will not have any real social impact\nD. The bias comes from the way the historical data were produced, and deleting the income field cannot eliminate it", back: "Answer: D\nA mistakenly believes that removing the sensitive field eliminates the bias, B treats the bias as an insufficient sample size, and C ignores the impact of algorithmic conclusions on real people.\n\nCorrect option: The bias comes from the way the historical data were produced, and deleting the income field cannot eliminate it" },
  "csp-scenario-016": { front: "A school plans to share student attendance data with a company for an \"absence reminder\" service, and the company will keep the raw data for a long time and use it to improve its own model. Which of the following deserves the most attention?\nA. The main risk of data sharing is slower transmission, which can be addressed by negotiating higher bandwidth with the company\nB. The data of students are kept for a long time and used a second time, and the students themselves can hardly withdraw them or know about it\nC. After the school signs a contract with the company, students are no longer stakeholders\nD. Attendance records are not personal information, so sharing them requires neither informing students nor obtaining consent", back: "Answer: B\nA mistakes a privacy risk for a bandwidth problem, C assumes that students are no longer stakeholders once a contract is signed, and D misjudges attendance data as not being personal information and thereby removes the duty to inform.\n\nCorrect option: The data of students are kept for a long time and used a second time, and the students themselves can hardly withdraw them or know about it" },
  "csp-scenario-017": { front: "A student uses binary search directly to find a value in an unsorted list of 1000 items. Which of the following is correct?\nA. Binary search works on any list, as long as its length is a power of 2\nB. The number of comparisons in binary search is independent of the list length, so it can always find the target in one step\nC. Binary search is a heuristic method: it is fast, but it cannot guarantee finding the target\nD. Binary search requires the list to be sorted; otherwise the search result may be incorrect", back: "Answer: D\nA replaces the precondition of being sorted with whether the length is a power of 2, B mistakenly believes that the number of comparisons is independent of the length and that the target can be found in one step, and C mistakes the deterministic binary search for a heuristic method.\n\nCorrect option: Binary search requires the list to be sorted; otherwise the search result may be incorrect" },
  "csp-scenario-018": { front: "A team simulates cafeteria queue times, assuming that each student takes exactly 30 seconds to order and not considering that students arrive at the same time during the peak between classes. Which of the following is correct?\nA. The simulation result is valid only when the assumptions hold, and ignoring the peak makes the prediction too optimistic\nB. The simulation uses random numbers, so it does not need verification; randomness automatically eliminates error\nC. As long as the number of simulation runs is increased, any wrong assumption will be corrected automatically\nD. A simulation is an experiment on the real system, and its conclusions are causal conclusions about the real world", back: "Answer: A\nB treats randomness as a reason to skip verification, C assumes that running many times can automatically correct wrong assumptions, and D takes simulation output directly as causal conclusions about the real world.\n\nCorrect option: The simulation result is valid only when the assumptions hold, and ignoring the peak makes the prediction too optimistic" },
  "csp-scenario-019": { front: "Which of the following is correct about the question of deciding whether an arbitrary program P halts on input I?\nA. The problem is undecidable, which means that no individual program can determine whether it itself halts\nB. The problem is only that no one has written an algorithm yet; once computing power improves it can be solved\nC. No general algorithm exists for this problem, but the running time of many specific programs can still be analyzed\nD. A heuristic method can give a correct decision for all inputs within a finite number of steps", back: "Answer: C\nA expands \"the general algorithm is undecidable\" into \"every individual program cannot be decided\", B mistakes undecidability for an engineering difficulty, and D mistakes a heuristic method for an algorithm that can guarantee a correct decision.\n\nCorrect option: No general algorithm exists for this problem, but the running time of many specific programs can still be analyzed" },
  "csp-scenario-020": { front: "A delivery company wants to find the shortest tour among 30 cities. The exact algorithm takes too long to run, so the company switches to a \"nearest neighbor + local adjustment\" method. Which of the following is correct?\nA. As long as it runs long enough, this method will eventually produce the optimal route\nB. This method can obtain a good route relatively quickly, but it is not guaranteed to be optimal\nC. The exact algorithm is too slow, which shows that no algorithm can solve the shortest tour problem\nD. Nearest neighbor is an exact algorithm, so the result it produces is necessarily optimal", back: "Answer: B\nA mistakenly believes that a heuristic method converges to the optimum if it simply runs longer, C mistakes a high computational cost for being unsolvable, and D mistakes the greedy strategy of nearest neighbor for an exact algorithm.\n\nCorrect option: This method can obtain a good route relatively quickly, but it is not guaranteed to be optimal" },
  "csp-scenario-021": { front: "A student builds a \"campus lost and found\" program: users submit an item description and a photo, and the program matches keywords in a list of 300 records and displays possible contact information. When writing the program purpose and input in the Create task report, which of the following is most appropriate?\nA. State that the program uses lists and procedures, so its purpose is to demonstrate data types\nB. Writing only \"this program is very useful and can help many people\" is enough, and the input does not need to be written\nC. State that the purpose of the program is to run without errors, and that the input is CPU and memory usage\nD. State that the purpose is to help match lost items with their owners, and that the input is the description and photo that the user fills in", back: "Answer: D\nA treats implementation details as the purpose, B gives only vague praise and lacks a description of the input, and C takes \"not reporting errors\" as the purpose and writes the wrong source of input.\n\nCorrect option: State that the purpose is to help match lost items with their owners, and that the input is the description and photo that the user fills in" },
  "csp-scenario-022": { front: "A student writes the procedure FIND(items, key), which returns the index of the first occurrence of key and returns -1 if it is not found, and calls it in several places in the program. When describing this procedure in the Create task report, which of the following is most appropriate?\nA. Explain the meaning of the parameters and the return value, and explain how it is called in the program\nB. Explain that the procedure is an abstraction, so the report does not need to state the meaning of the parameters and return value\nC. Explain line by line the syntax of each statement inside the procedure, so that readers can copy it\nD. Explain that the procedure uses a loop, so it is data abstraction rather than procedural abstraction", back: "Answer: A\nB misunderstands abstraction as allowing the description of the interface to be omitted, C turns the report into a line-by-line explanation of syntax, and D confuses procedural abstraction with data abstraction.\n\nCorrect option: Explain the meaning of the parameters and the return value, and explain how it is called in the program" },
  "csp-scenario-023": { front: "A student's program passes the tests for cases 1, 2, and 3, but it crashes when the input is an empty list. According to the Create task testing requirements, which of the following is most appropriate?\nA. As long as all three cases pass, the report can state that the program is correct\nB. Record the input and expected result of each case, add the empty-list case, and modify the code\nC. The crash shows that this is only a syntax error, and the case should be deleted from the report so that it does not affect the score\nD. As long as the whole program is rewritten, there is no need to record the test data used before", back: "Answer: B\nA equates passing three test cases with the program being correct, C misjudges the empty-list crash as a syntax error and wants to hide the case, and D assumes that the test records can be omitted after the code is rewritten.\n\nCorrect option: Record the input and expected result of each case, add the empty-list case, and modify the code" },
  "csp-scenario-024": { front: "A student's program uses background music downloaded from the internet, a photo of a classmate, and 200 user comments scraped from a public website. Which of the following is most appropriate?\nA. Content that is public online is in the public domain, so it can be used directly without citing the source\nB. As long as the name of the music is written in the report, licensing and personal data need not be considered\nC. Use licensed material and cite the sources, and at the same time protect the personal information in the comments\nD. Scraping public comments is using public data, so there is no privacy problem at all", back: "Answer: C\nA mistakes being publicly visible for being in the public domain, B only provides attribution while ignoring licensing and personal information, and D mistakenly believes that public data raise no privacy problems.\n\nCorrect option: Use licensed material and cite the sources, and at the same time protect the personal information in the comments" },
  "csp-scenario-025": { front: "A student writes \"count the total steps of each student this week\" as the procedure TOTAL(steps, days) and calls it in several places in the main program. Which of the following is correct about abstraction and the content of the report?\nA. A procedure is an abstraction, so the report does not need to state who the intended users of the program are\nB. Because the procedure is called many times, it is a simulation rather than a kind of abstraction\nC. The variable names inside the procedure must match those at the call site, otherwise parameter passing will fail\nD. A procedure with parameters is an abstraction, and the report still needs to state the intended users and purpose", back: "Answer: D\nA assumes that using abstraction means no user description is needed, B mistakes procedural abstraction for a simulation, and C invents a requirement that parameter passing places on variable names.\n\nCorrect option: A procedure with parameters is an abstraction, and the report still needs to state the intended users and purpose" },
};
